description:
Write a small part of what `InfVerif` checks (lesson 5.04): read the `[Version]` section of an INF file, validate it, and compare `DriverVer` values.

```cpp
struct DriverVer { int month, day, year; int v[4]; };
struct VersionInfo { std::string signature, cls, provider, catalog; DriverVer ver; };

bool parseDriverVer(const std::string& text, DriverVer& out);             // "11/15/2026,1.0.0.0"
std::vector<std::string> checkVersion(const std::string& inf, VersionInfo& out);
int compareDriverVer(const DriverVer& a, const DriverVer& b);            // -1, 0, 1: date first, then version
```

**The INF**: lines (`\r\n` allowed); `;` starts a comment to the end of the line; blank lines are ignored; `[Name]` starts a section (names are case-insensitive); in a section, `key = value` with spaces around keys and values trimmed and keys compared ignoring case. Only `[Version]` matters here.

**parseDriverVer**: `MM/DD/YYYY,a.b.c.d` with a valid date (month 1-12, day within the month, leap years counted, year 1900 or later), and four version parts 0-65535; the version part may also have fewer parts (`1.2`), the rest being 0. No spaces.

**checkVersion** returns the problems found, in this order, or an empty list:
- `"no [Version] section"` (then nothing else is checked);
- `"Signature must be $WINDOWS NT$"` unless it is exactly that, ignoring case and with or without surrounding quotes;
- `"missing Class"`, `"missing Provider"`, `"missing CatalogFile"` for each one that is absent or empty;
- `"bad DriverVer"` if absent or invalid.

It fills `out` with what it found (quotes around the signature removed).

